<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-sets-set-operations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Sets and Set Operations</title>

	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use set notation and understand the empty set.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Determine the universal set for a given context.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Perform basic set operations (union, intersection, and complement).
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use Venn diagrams and set notation to illustrate the intersection, union, and complements of sets.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			The idea of a <term>set</term> allows us to treat a group of distinct objects as a single entity. For example, we could use <m>S</m> to represent the four seasons: spring, summer, fall, and winter.  That is,
			<md>
				S = \left\{\text{spring, summer, fall, winter} \right\}.
			</md>
			By classifying items as groups (sets), we can also investigate relationships between groups, or consider overlap between groups when counting items.
		</p>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A <term>set</term><idx>sets</idx> is an unordered collection of distinct objects, called the <term>elements</term><idx><h>sets</h><h>element</h></idx> (or <term>members</term><idx><h>member (of a set)</h><see>sets, element</see></idx>) of the set.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
	</introduction>

	<subsection>
		<title>Set Notation</title>
		<p>
			A set can be defined by describing its contents or by listing its elements. Sets are usually named with capital letters, and braces { } are used to enclose the set's elements. 
			The method of listing the elements of a set in braces is called <term>roster notation</term><idx>roster notation</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>roster notation</h></idx>. A second method uses <term>set-builder notation</term><idx>set-builder notation</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>set-builder notation</h></idx>, where braces and a vertical line | (read <q>such that</q>) describe the set's contents.
		</p>
		<p>
			To say that an object <m>x</m> is an element of a set <m>A</m>, we write <m>x \in A</m>; if it is not, we write <m>x \notin A</m>. For example, <m>4 \in \{0,2,4,6,8\}</m>, but <m>5 \notin \{0,2,4,6,8\}</m>.
		</p>
		<example>
			<p>
				Describing a set by its contents:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							The even digits.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The colors of the rainbow.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			<p>
				Roster notation:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>E = \{0,2,4,6,8\}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>C = \{\text{red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet} \}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			<p>
				Set-builder notation:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>E = \{x \mid x \text{ is an even digit}\}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>C = \{x \mid x \text{ is a color of the rainbow}\}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</example>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Julia, Keenan, Jae, and Colin took a test. They received the following scores: 70, 95, 85, and 70. Let <m>P</m> be the set of test takers and <m>S</m> be the set of test scores. List the elements of each set using roster notation.
			</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					In this example, the set of people taking the test is <m>P= \{\text{Julia, Keenan, Jae, Colin}\}</m>, and the set of test scores is <m>S = \{70, 85, 95\}</m>. Notice that even though two people scored a 70 on the test, the score of 70 is listed only once, as elements in a set are unique.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			Sometimes a collection might not contain all the elements of a set.  For example, suppose Chris owns three Taylor Swift albums.  While Chris's collection is a set, we can also say it is a subset of the larger set of all Taylor Swift albums.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-subset">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two sets. Then <m>A</m> is a <term>subset</term><idx>subset</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>subset</h></idx> of <m>B</m>, written <m>A\subseteq B</m>, if and only if every element of <m>A</m> is also an element of <m>B</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two sets. We say that <m>A</m> is <term>equal</term><idx><h>sets</h><h>equal</h></idx> to <m>B</m>, written <m>A = B</m>, if and only if <m>A\subseteq B</m> and <m>B\subseteq A</m>. This means that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> contain the same elements.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<example>
			<p>
				Consider the three sets
				<md>
					A = \{1,2,3\},\quad B = \{1,2,3,4,5\},\quad C = \{3,2,1\}.
				</md>
				Then each of the following set relationships is true:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p><m>A \subseteq B</m></p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><m>A = C</m></p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><m>A \subseteq C</m></p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</example>
		<p>
			It is possible to have a set with nothing in it. This set is called the <term>null set</term><idx><h>null set</h><see>empty set</see></idx> or <term>empty set</term>. It is like going to the grocery store to buy your favorite foods and realizing you left your wallet at home. 
			You walk away with an empty bag. The set of items that you bought at the grocery store would be written in set notation as <m>\emptyset</m>. The empty set is a subset of every set.
		</p>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The set that contains no elements is called the <term>empty set</term><idx>empty set</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>empty set</h></idx> and is denoted <m>\emptyset</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<p>
			In applications of sets, the elements that may be considered are often limited to some context, or <term>universe of discourse</term><idx><h>universe of discourse</h><see>universal set</see></idx>. This context is called the <term>universal set</term> and is the set containing every possible element of the described context. Every set is thus a subset of the universal set.
		</p>
		<p>
			When represented visually, the universal set is often illustrated by a rectangle labeled with a capital letter <m>U</m>. Subsets of the universal set are usually illustrated with circles for simplicity, but other shapes can be used. The visual representation of sets is further discussed in <xref text="title" ref="subsec-sets-venn-diagrams"/>.
		</p>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The <term>universal set</term><idx>universal set</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>universal set</h></idx> <m>U</m> is defined to be the set of all elements under consideration.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<example>
			<p>
				What the universal set contains depends on context and is sometimes subject to interpretation. Here are some examples.
			</p>
			<p>
			<ol marker="a)">
				<li>
					<sidebyside valign="middle" width="40%">
						<p>
							If you are searching for books for a research project, the universal set might be all the books in the library, and the books in the library that are relevant to your research project would be a subset of the universal set.
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-set-ex1" width="100%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U, labeled all books in the library; there is a circle inside the rectangle, labeled books relevant to your research project.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
									% Define the universe as a rectangle
									\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,4);

									% Add label for the universe inside the box
									\node[align=center] at (4.65,2) {U = All books \\ in the library};

									% Draw the yellow circle for research-relevant books
									\filldraw[fill=yellow!70, draw=black, thick] (1.75,2) circle (1.5);

									% Add label for the research books - on four lines
									\node[align=center] at (1.75,2) {Books \\ relevant to \\ your research \\ project};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</sidebyside>
				</li>
				<li>
					<sidebyside valign="middle" width="40%">
						<p>
							If you want to create a group of your Facebook friends who are coworkers, the universal set would be all your Facebook friends and the group of coworkers would be a subset of the universal set.
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-set-ex2" width="100%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U, labeled "All your Facebook friends"; there is a circle inside the rectangle, labeled "Coworkers".</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
									% Define the universe as a rectangle
									\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,4);
							
									% Add label for the universe inside the box
									\node[align=center] at (4.65,2) {U = All your \\ Facebook \\ friends};
							
									% Draw the yellow circle for the subset
									\filldraw[fill=yellow!70, draw=black, thick] (1.75,2) circle (1.5);
							
									% Add label for the subset
									\node[align=center] at (1.75,2) {Coworkers};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</sidebyside>
				</li>
				<li>
					<sidebyside valign="middle" width="40%">
						<p>
							If you are working with sets of numbers, the universal set might be all whole numbers, and the set of all prime numbers would be a subset of the universal set.
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-set-ex3" width="100%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U, labeled "All whole numbers"; there is a circle inside the rectangle, labeled "All prime numbers".</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
									% Define the universe as a rectangle
									\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,4);
							
									% Add label for the universe inside the box
									\node[align=center] at (4.65,2) {U = All whole \\ numbers};
							
									% Draw the yellow circle for the subset
									\filldraw[fill=yellow!70, draw=black, thick] (1.75,2) circle (1.5);
							
									% Add label for the subset
									\node[align=center] at (1.75,2) {All prime \\numbers};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</sidebyside>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-set-operations">
		<title>Set Operations: Union, Intersection, and Complement</title>
		<p>
			Suppose you and your roommate decide to have a house party, and you each invite your circle, or set, of friends. When you combine your two sets of friends, you discover that you have some friends in common.
		</p>
		<p>
			The set of friends that you have in common is called the <term>intersection</term>. The intersection of two sets contains only the elements that are in both sets. An element must be in both set <m>A</m> <alert>and</alert> set <m>B</m> to be in their intersection.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-intersection">
	  	<idx>intersection</idx>
	  	<idx><h>sets</h><h>intersection</h></idx>
	  	<statement>
	  		<p>
					The <term>intersection</term> of two sets, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, denoted <m>A \cap B</m>, is the set containing all elements that are in both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.  That is,
					<md>
						A \cap B = \{x\mid x\in A \text{ AND } x\in B \}.
					</md>
				</p>
	  	</statement>
	  </definition>
		<p>
			The set of all friends that you and your roommate have invited is called the <term>union</term>. The union of two sets contains all the elements contained in either set (or both). To be in the union of set <m>A</m> and set <m>B</m>, an element must be contained in just set <m>A</m>, just set <m>B</m>, <alert>or</alert> in the intersection of sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. Notice that in this case the <q>or</q> is <alert>inclusive</alert>.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-union">
	  	<idx>union</idx>
	  	<idx><h>sets</h><h>union</h></idx>
	  	<statement>
	  		<p>
					The <term>union</term> of two sets, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, denoted <m>A\cup B</m>, is the set containing all elements that are in <m>A</m> or in <m>B</m> (or both). That is,
					<md>
						A \cup B = \{x\mid x\in A \text{ OR } x\in B \}.
					</md>
				</p>
	  	</statement>
	  </definition>
		<p>
			What about the people who were <em>not</em> invited to the party and showed up anyway? They are not elements of your set of invited friends. Nor are they elements of your roommate's set of invited friends. These uninvited party crashers are in the <term>complement</term> of the set of all invited friends. The complement of a set <m>A</m> contains everything that is <em>not</em> in the set <m>A</m>. To be in the complement of set <m>A</m>, an element <alert>cannot</alert> be in set <m>A</m>, but it will be an element of the universal set.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-complement">
  		<idx>complement</idx>
  		<idx><h>sets</h><h>complement</h></idx>
  		<statement>
  			<p>
					The <term>complement</term> of a set <m>A</m> in a universal set <m>U</m> is the set <m>A^C</m> consisting of all elements in <m>U</m> that are not in <m>A</m>. That is,
					<md>
						A^{C} = \{x\in U\mid x \notin A \}.
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					<alert>Note:</alert> The complement of a set <m>A</m> may also be denoted by <m>A'</m> or <m>\overline{A}</m>.
				</p>
  		</statement>
  	</definition>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Consider the sets: <m>A = \{\text{red, green, blue}\}</m>, <m>B = \{\text{red, yellow, orange}\}</m>, and <m>C = \{\text{red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet}\}</m>.
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							Determine <m>A \cap B </m>, and write it in set notation.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Determine <m>A \cup B</m>, and write it in set notation.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Determine <m>A^C \cap C</m> and write it in set notation.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								The intersection contains the elements in both sets: <m>A \cap B = \{\text{red}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The union contains all the elements in either set: <m>A \cup B = \{\text{red, green, blue, yellow, orange}\}</m>. Notice we only list red once.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Here we are looking for all the elements that are <em>not</em> in set <m>A</m> and are in set <m>C</m>: <m>A^C \cap C = \{\text{orange, yellow, indigo, violet}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-sets-venn-diagrams">
		<title>Venn Diagrams</title>
		<p>
			<term>Venn diagrams</term> are used to illustrate the relationships between two or more sets. To create a Venn diagram, start by drawing a rectangle to represent the universal set. Next draw and label overlapping circles to represent each of your sets. Most often there will be two or three sets illustrated in a Venn diagram. Finally, if elements are given, fill in each region with its corresponding elements. Venn diagrams are also a great way to illustrate intersections, unions, and complements of sets, as shown below.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-venn-diagram">
	  	<idx>Venn diagram</idx>
	  	<idx><h>sets</h><h>Venn diagram</h></idx>
	  	<statement>
	  		<p>
					A <term>Venn diagram</term> is a picture used to represent sets and their relationships.  Each set is represented by a circle, with the area inside the circle representing the elements that are in the set.  The entire diagram is enclosed in a rectangle representing the set of all possible elements, called the <term>universal set</term>.
				</p>
	  	</statement>
	  </definition>
		<sidebyside widths="33% 33% 33%">
			<figure>
				<caption>The <alert>intersection</alert> is the shaded region where the two sets overlap. It contains the elements in <m>A</m> <alert>and</alert> <m>B</m>.</caption>
				<image xml:id="img-set-intersection" width="100%">
				<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U; only the region where the two circles overlap is shaded, showing the intersection.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
						% Define the universe as a rectangle
						\draw[thin] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
						% Draw A
						\draw[fill=white] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
						% Draw B
						\draw[fill=white] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
						% Intersection area
						\begin{scope}
								\clip (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
								\fill[yellow!70] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
						\end{scope}
				
						% Labels
						\node[align=center] at (0.6,1) {$A$};
						\node[align=center] at (2.025,1) {$B$};
						\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
				
						% Draw borders
						\draw[draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
						\draw[draw=black, thin] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
				</image>
      </figure>
			<figure>
				<caption>The <alert>union</alert> includes all elements in <m>A</m> <alert>or</alert> <m>B</m> or both. It is the entire shaded region.</caption>
				<image xml:id="img-set-union" width="100%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U; both circles, including their overlap, are shaded to show the union.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
							% Define the universe as a rectangle
							\draw[thin] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
							% Draw A
							\draw[fill=yellow!70] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
							% Draw B
							\draw[fill=yellow!70] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
							% Labels
							\node[align=center] at (0.6,1) {$A$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.025,1) {$B$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
					
							% Draw borders
							\draw[draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
							\draw[draw=black, thin] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</figure>
			<figure>
				<caption>The <alert>complement</alert> of set <m>A</m> includes all the elements <alert>not</alert> in <m>A</m>. It is the shaded region outside <m>A</m>, but within the universal set.</caption>
				<image xml:id="img-set-complement" width="100%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram with only one set A; The region outside of circle A but inside the rectangle is shaded to represent the complement of the set, or elements not in set A.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
							% Define the universe as a rectangle
							\draw[thin, fill=yellow!70] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
							% Draw A
							\filldraw[fill=white, draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
							% labels
							\node[align=center] at (0.875,1) {$A$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.125,1) {$A^C$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</figure>
	  </sidebyside>
		<example xml:id="ex-venn-diagram-construction">
			<title>Constructing a Venn Diagram</title>	  	
			<statement>
				<p>Let <m>A = \lbrace a, c, d, e \rbrace</m> and <m>B = \lbrace d, e, f \rbrace</m> be subsets of the universal set <m>U = \lbrace a, b, c, d, e, f \rbrace</m>.  Draw a Venn diagram representing these sets.</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>Draw a box to represent <m>U</m> and one circle for each of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, making sure that there is a region inside both circles.  Then place the elements in the appropriate region as shown below.</p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-ex-venn-diagram-const-letters">
					<caption>Venn diagram of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> in <m>U</m>.</caption>
					<image width="45%" xml:id="img-ex-venn-diagram-const-letters">
						<shortdescription>
							A Venn diagram with two circles, one for A and one for B, which overlap in the middle.  Elements a and c are in circle A but not circle B, element f is in circle B but not circle A, elements d and e are in both circles, and element b is not in either circle.
						</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
								\draw[fill=white, thin] (-1.5,-1.5) rectangle (2.5,1.5);
								\draw[fill=white, thin] (0,0) circle (1);
								\draw[fill=white, thin] (1,0) circle (1);
								\draw (0,0) circle (1); % outline
								\node at (-1,1) {$A$};
								\node at (2,1) {$B$};							  
								\node at (0.5,0.35)  {\small $d$};
								\node at (0.5,-0.35) {\small $e$};
								\node at (-0.5,0.1)  {\small $a$};
								\node at (-0.3,-0.3) {\small $c$};
								\node at (1.4,0)     {\small $f$};
								\node at (2,-1.2)  {\small $b$};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Create a Venn diagram to illustrate <m>A^C \cap B</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The set <m>A^C</m> contains all elements not in set <m>A</m>. This means that <m>A^C \cap B</m> contains elements that are not in <m>A</m> and are in <m>B</m>. That is, it contains the elements of <m>B</m> that are not in <m>A</m> as shown in the following Venn diagram.
				</p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-venn-ex-with-complement">
					<caption>A Venn diagram illustrating the set <m>A^C \cap B</m> shaded in yellow.</caption>
					<image width="45%" xml:id="img-venn-ex-with-complement">
						<shortdescription>
							A Venn diagram with two circles, one for A and one for B, which overlap in the middle.  The shaded region is a crescent shape in B that is not in A.
						</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
								% Define the universe as a rectangle
								\draw[thin,fill=white] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
								% Draw A
								\draw[fill=white] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
								% Draw B
								\draw[fill=white] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
								% A complement intersect B area
								\begin{scope}
										\clip (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
										\fill[yellow!70] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
										\begin{scope}
												\clip (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
												\fill[white] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
										\end{scope}
								\end{scope}
						
								% Labels
								\node[align=center] at (0.6,1) {$A$};
								\node[align=center] at (2.025,1) {$B$};
								\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
						
								% Draw borders
								\draw[draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
								\draw[draw=black, thin] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
</section>